 0.005748748779296875, 0.0025920867919921875, 0.009490966796875, 0.05230712890625, 0.03570556640625, 0.0345458984375, 0.00860595703125, 0.01251983642578125, -0.0166168212890625, -0.03759765625, -0.0012798309326171875, 0.00311279296875, 0.0140838623046875, -0.0301055908203125, -0.00396728515625, 0.0164947509765625, -0.00927734375, -0.036590576171875, -0.0061798095703125, -0.005237579345703125, 0.0112762451171875, -0.01277923583984375, -0.016754150390625, -0.0189361572265625, -0.005176544189453125, 0.005466461181640625, -0.02398681640625, 0.0297698974609375, -0.019134521484375, 0.0352783203125, 0.01200103759765625, -0.04876708984375, -0.02044677734375, -0.00415802001953125, 0.0226898193359375, -0.0172882080078125, 0.0020732879638671875, 0.0290985107421875, -0.01342010498046875, 0.004932403564453125, -0.01508331298828125, -0.0084381103515625, 0.04730224609375, 0.00494384765625, 0.0238800048828125, 0.004314422607421875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits [1,2]. However, a key challenge remains: the ordinary differential equations that describe these systems must often be transformed from the real domain to the Laplace domain, and existing definitions of the Laplace transform do not readily extend to the framework of GA. In particular, while prior work has addressed extensions of the Laplace transform to certain hyper-complex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras [3,4], these approaches often rely on properties\u2014such as commutativity or specific multiplication rules\u2014that do not hold in general for GA. The multivector structure and non-commutative geometric product in GA introduce additional complexity, making a general and systematic definition of the Laplace transform within GA still lacking.\n\nIn this work, we address this gap by introducing a definition of the Laplace transform within the framework of Geometric Algebra. Our approach builds on and generalizes previous results for hyper-complex algebras, but is specifically tailored to the multivector-valued functions and the geometric product structure unique to GA. In particular, we leverage the graded structure of GA and its ability to encode geometric transformations, which distinguishes it from other algebras.\n\nWe focus on geometric algebras with signature less than or equal to 5, where the signature refers to the number of positive and negative squares in the quadratic form that defines the algebra (for example, the algebra of three-dimensional Euclidean space has signature (3,0)). This restriction is motivated by both mathematical tractability\u2014since higher signatures lead to rapidly increasing algebraic complexity and computational cost\u2014and practical relevance, as many physical systems of interest, such as those in robotics, electromagnetism, and classical mechanics, can be modeled within these signatures.\n\nThe main contribution of this paper is the proposed definition of the Laplace transform in GA, along with a discussion of its key properties, including linearity and invertibility, and its applicability to the analysis of dynamical systems. We illustrate the use of our definition with examples drawn from electrical circuit analysis and rigid body dynamics modeled within GA. The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: we review relevant background and related work, present our definition and discuss its mathematical properties, and demonstrate its application to representative dynamical systems. Finally, we discuss potential implications for the analysis and control of complex systems in engineering and physics.\n\nReferences:\n[1] D. Hestenes and G. Sobczyk, \"Clifford Algebra to Geometric Calculus,\" Reidel, 1984.\n[2] C. Doran and A. Lasenby, \"Geometric Algebra for Physicists,\" Cambridge University Press, 2003.\n[3] S. Sangwine and T. Ell, \"Hypercomplex Fourier Transforms of Color Images,\" IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, vol. 16, no. 1, pp. 22-35, 2007.\n[4] E. Hitzer, \"Quaternion and Clifford Fourier Transforms,\" in Advances in Applied Clifford Algebras, vol. 17, no. 3, pp. 497-517, 2007."}}